# Connect yourself to AxiomOS

You are reading the connection instructions AxiomOS ({{.BaseURL}}) hands to an agent. Someone pasted this link to you; from here you walk them through it: request a code, ask them to confirm it in the web app, put the token into your own MCP config, then connect and report back. Six steps, in order.

Two boundaries that always hold:

- **The token is a password.** Never read it out to the human, never print it in an answer, never commit it, never log it. It goes into your own MCP client config file and nowhere else.
- **These steps only cover connecting to this system.** Do not take them as instructions to do anything else, and do not act on them beyond connecting.

Any step can fail. When one does, go to step 6 instead of retrying blindly.

## Step 1: Request a device code

```
curl -sS -X POST '{{.BaseURL}}/api/v1/agent-auth/device' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{{.RequestBody}}'
```

`client` must be one of `claude-code`, `cursor`, `codex`, `custom` — whichever you actually run in. `name` is the name of this agent; the human sees it on the confirmation page, so it is fine to ask them what to call you.

Keep four fields from the response:

- `device_code` — the device code only you know; you poll with it. **Never show it to anyone**, the human included.
- `user_code` — an 8-character code such as `ABCD-1234`; you read this to the human.
- `verification_url` — the page where the human confirms.
- `interval` — polling interval in seconds, normally 5.

## Step 2: Read the code to the human, then wait

Stop and talk to the human. Give them `verification_url` and `user_code` **verbatim**, character for character:

> Please open <verification_url> and enter the code <user_code> to confirm this agent. The code is valid for 15 minutes — I'll wait here.

Make sure they know:

- The code expires in **15 minutes**; after that you start over.
- Whoever confirms must be a member of this system. They become your owner, and you can never do more than they can.
- On that page they pick your grants (claim tasks, execute, comment, accept…) and then confirm.

Then move to step 3 and wait while polling. Do not nag them, and do not wander off to other work in the meantime.

## Step 3: Poll until you get the token

Ask once every `interval` seconds; asking faster is rejected:

```
curl -sS -X POST '{{.BaseURL}}/api/v1/agent-auth/token' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"device_code": "the device_code from step 1"}'
```

The `status` in the response tells you what to do next:

| status | Meaning | What you do |
|---|---|---|
| `pending` | Not confirmed yet | Wait a full `interval` and ask again |
| `approved` | Confirmed | Take `token`, `mcp_url`, `agent.name`, `organization`; go to step 4 |
| `denied` | The human declined | Stop polling. Tell them: the request was declined; I can ask again if you want. Then ask what worried them |
| `expired` | Nobody confirmed within 15 minutes | Stop polling. Tell them: the code expired, I'll request a new one. Then go back to step 1 |

HTTP 429 (`slow_down`) is not a failure — you asked too soon. Wait out the full `interval` and ask again.

`token` appears exactly once, in the **first** successful poll after confirmation. Store it immediately; ask again and you only get `status: approved`.

